 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491978 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491978 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186491473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491978 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eef1360f-bfc8-557c-ac1f-fead556c6f63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491980 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491980 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491980 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491980 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491980 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186491717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491980 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491980 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491980 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186491598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491980 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f53007b8-0f0f-5ce4-9896-cf1efd1f672a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491984 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491984 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491984 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491984 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=1$ and $det(A)<0$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is negative-definite" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491984 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06dbce8a-d24c-5a95-a85b-452eb260bee0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491984 #edb/ref 1074 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that ${a}_{11}=1,$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=1>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491989 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491989 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94794" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491989 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491989 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is indefinite" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491989 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0b664be8-b1be-541d-ac3b-8edf1dd9084b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491989 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -21 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491990 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491990 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94791" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491990 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491990 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are indefinite?\n\nI. ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$\n\nIII. ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491990 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10c8a343-4eff-5fd9-bb35-1f19e420eeaf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491990 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is indefinite. Indeed, $Q$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* indefinite since all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are negative.\n\n- Quadratic form III is indefinite. Indeed, $S$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491992 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491992 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94738" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491992 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491992 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\nI. $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}$ is positive-definite\n\nII. $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x$ is indefinite\n\nIII. $T(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}$ is negative-definite" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491992 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "128edce0-e99a-5468-9781-896334433102" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491992 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that, a quadratic form $Q(x)$ is:\n\n- positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖\\{0\\};$\n\n- negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖\\{0\\};$\n\n- indefinite if \n\n- $Q({x}_{1})>0$ for some ${x}_{1}\\in {R}^{n},$ and\n\n- $Q({x}_{2})<0$ for some ${x}_{2}\\in {R}^{n}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖\\{0\\},$ the quadratic form is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting ${x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S({x}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ${x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S({x}_{2}) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic form is indefinite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖\\{0\\},$ the quadratic form is negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492017 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492017 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139438" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492017 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492017 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are positive semi-definite? \n\nI. ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n\nIII. ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492017 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a462168-8926-51f0-99cc-2ed457f027f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492017 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is *not* positive semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are negative (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ is negative).\n\n- Quadratic form II is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are non-negative.\n\n- Quadratic form III is positive semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492023 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492023 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139434" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492023 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492023 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\nI. $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$\n\nII. $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]x$\n\nIII. $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492023 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c5d19b4-6f2c-5705-8395-4e5ce1c9092d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492023 #edb/ref 1074 "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖\\{0\\}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =1+9+6 \\\\  & =16>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =16>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be positive-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={1}^{2}-(-3)^{2}+1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =1-9-3 \\\\  & =-11<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492064 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492064 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94733" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492064 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492064 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be negative-definite.\n\nI. $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}$\n\nII. $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]x$\n\nIII. $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492064 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ddb328b6-849a-5813-b5a8-23aaa33d5ab6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492064 #edb/ref 1074 "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖\\{0\\}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *cannot* be negative-definite since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={5}^{2}+2(0)^{2} \\\\  & =25+0 \\\\  & =25>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *might* be negative-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-50<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *cannot* be negative-definite since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={5}^{2}-{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =25+0 \\\\  & =25>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
